@title How a Finished Lab Result Is Routed
@subtitle Who gets told, and when
@author Laboratory Informatics, Calder Valley Hospital
@date 2026-10-02

For new unit clerks and nurses. Critical values are urgent and need a phone call within minutes. Abnormal and routine results follow slower, scheduled steps.

## Routing flow

diagram routing dir=TB title="Path of a verified lab result"
routing: start "Result verified in lab system" svc
routing: crit "Critical value?" decision
routing: phone "Phone the ordering clinician" svc "Urgent" focus
routing: readback "Read back confirmed within 15 minutes?" decision
routing: log "Log the read-back" svc
routing: esc "Escalate to department head" svc "Urgent" focus
routing: abn "Flagged abnormal?" decision
routing: inbox "Add to clinician inbox" svc
routing: file "File without alert" svc
routing: release "Release to patient portal" ext
routing: start -> crit
routing: crit -> phone : yes
routing: phone -> readback
routing: readback -> log : yes
routing: readback -> esc : no
routing: esc -> log
routing: crit -> abn : no
routing: abn -> inbox : yes
routing: abn -> file : no
routing: inbox -> release
routing: file -> release
routing: legend

## Time limits

table = Step,Limit,Urgency; Phone call for a critical value,within 10 minutes of verification,**Urgent**; Read-back confirmation,within 15 minutes of the call,**Urgent**; Abnormal result review in clinician inbox,within 1 business day,Routine; Routine result released to patient portal,after 24 hours,Routine; Abnormal result held before release,72 hours so the clinician can speak to the patient first,Routine

## Who is called

kpi Weekdays 07:00 to 19:00 | The ordering clinician
kpi Nights and weekends | Covering physician, through the hospital operator
kpi Department head | Dr. Naomi Eze
